["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Know where the heart, lungs and air sacs sit, and you know why some hit ducks drop and others fly off to die.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view cutaway of a duck facing right. The spine runs along the top. The small lungs lie high against the spine and ribs, under the wing roots. Air sacs fill the rear of the body and sit in front by the neck. The heart sits forward and low, behind the keel. The liver is below the lungs. The gizzard is lower and further back. The big breast muscle covers the keel. Heart and lungs sit high and forward, 75 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Inside a duck: side view</text><path d=\"M50 150 Q48 105 110 92 L250 86 Q300 84 318 100 Q332 70 350 62 Q376 56 382 76 L396 82 L382 88 Q372 96 352 104 Q340 130 330 160 Q318 214 240 222 L120 222 Q60 214 50 150 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M70 110 Q160 92 300 100\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M200 214 Q270 214 318 168 L330 150\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M210 212 Q272 210 316 166 L300 150 Q250 176 214 176 Z\" fill=\"#c96b5a\"/><ellipse cx=\"135\" cy=\"140\" rx=\"55\" ry=\"26\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"122\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"236\" cy=\"114\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"11\" fill=\"#e7a3a3\" stroke=\"#b5473a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M276 140 Q290 136 292 150 Q290 166 278 172 Q266 160 266 148 Q268 138 276 140Z\" fill=\"#b5473a\"/><ellipse cx=\"236\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#7a3b22\"/><ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"186\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"17\" fill=\"#9a6a3a\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M96 170 q12 -14 24 0 q12 14 24 0\" stroke=\"#a0805a\" stroke-width=\"6\" fill=\"none\"/><line x1=\"236\" y1=\"114\" x2=\"236\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"236\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lungs</text><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"98\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"98\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Air sacs</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Air sacs</text><line x1=\"282\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"352\" y2=\"182\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heart</text><line x1=\"236\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"252\" y2=\"248\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"252\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Liver</text><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"248\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gizzard</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"196\" x2=\"352\" y2=\"222\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"236\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Breast</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spine</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heart and lungs sit high and forward (75%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Heart and lungs: high and forward, under the wing roots <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>The back half is air sacs and gut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the parts that matter to a hunter</summary><ul><li>Lungs: small, stiff, pressed up against the spine and ribs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Air sacs: thin bags in front of and behind the lungs, filling much of the body. They move air but do not take up oxygen <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Heart: big for the body, low and forward in the chest, behind the keel <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Breast: the big flight muscle on the keel. This is most of the meat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Gizzard: a hard muscle pouch low in the belly, behind the liver <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Spine and neck: a pellet here drops the bird at once (Tip, from the vital areas in chapter 10).</li><li>Anatomy positions are drawn from general bird anatomy, simplified <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation of a bird breathing. A blue dot of fresh air comes down the windpipe. Step 1, breathe in: the fresh air goes past the lung into the rear air sacs. Step 2, breathe out: the rear sacs push it forward through the lung, where oxygen is taken up and the dot turns grey. Step 3, breathe in: the used air moves into the front sacs. Step 4, breathe out: it leaves by the windpipe. End card: fresh air crosses the lung on the in breath and the out breath, one way flow, 80 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Two breaths move one gulp of air (80%)</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"120\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7a3a3\" stroke=\"#b5473a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lung</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"110\" width=\"100\" height=\"80\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rear sacs</text><rect x=\"280\" y=\"110\" width=\"100\" height=\"80\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Front sacs</text><rect x=\"300\" y=\"40\" width=\"24\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e9e2d0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Windpipe</text><g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"0s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"2.5s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"214\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"237\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Breathe in: fresh air to rear sacs</text></g><g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"2.5s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"5.0s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"214\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"237\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Breathe out: rear sacs push it through the lung</text></g><g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"5s\"/><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"0\" begin=\"7.5s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"214\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"237\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. Breathe in: used air to front sacs</text></g><g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"7.5s\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"214\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"237\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Breathe out: it leaves</text></g><path id=\"mdk-breath-p\" d=\"M312 44 L312 100 L150 100 L70 120 L70 150 L120 150 L200 150 L280 150 L330 150 L330 110 L312 100 L312 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 6\" opacity=\".6\"/><circle r=\"11\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke=\"#ffffff\" stroke-width=\"2\"><animateMotion dur=\"10s\" fill=\"freeze\" keyPoints=\"0;0.32;0.32;0.62;0.62;0.82;0.82;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.22;0.25;0.47;0.5;0.72;0.75;1\" calcMode=\"linear\" path=\"M312 44 L312 100 L150 100 L70 120 L70 150 L120 150 L200 150 L280 150 L330 150 L330 110 L312 100 L312 40\"/><animate attributeName=\"fill\" values=\"#4a7fb0;#4a7fb0;#8c939b;#8c939b\" keyTimes=\"0;0.4;0.5;1\" dur=\"10s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></circle><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"10.2s\" dur=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"258\" width=\"380\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fresh air crosses the lung on in AND out breaths</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption>Two breaths move one gulp of air. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Air flows ONE way through a bird lung <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>It takes two breaths to move one gulp through <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the two breath cycle in plain words</summary><ul><li>Breath 1 in: fresh air goes past the lung, mostly into the rear air sacs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Breath 1 out: the rear sacs squeeze that fresh air forward through the lung <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Breath 2 in: the used air moves on into the front air sacs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Breath 2 out: it leaves through the windpipe <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>So the lung gets fresh air on the in breath and the out breath. Bird lungs get oxygen better than ours, even high up <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>No diaphragm: the ribs and breastbone pump the air sacs like bellows <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: a duck can fly hard for hours. A lightly hit duck can still fly a long way before it falls (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Map of the 9 air sacs in a duck facing right, 80 percent: 2 neck sacs, 1 collar sac at the base of the neck, 2 front chest sacs, 2 rear chest sacs and 2 big belly sacs at the back. The lung is small and stiff, high against the spine. Air from the sacs reaches into the hollow wing bones.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">9 air sacs, 2 small stiff lungs</text><path d=\"M50 150 Q48 105 110 92 L250 86 Q300 84 318 100 Q332 70 350 62 Q376 56 382 76 L396 82 L382 88 Q372 96 352 104 Q340 130 330 160 Q318 214 240 222 L120 222 Q60 214 50 150 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"330\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"306\" cy=\"128\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"262\" cy=\"146\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"214\" cy=\"152\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"58\" ry=\"30\" fill=\"#bcd8f0\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"240\" cy=\"112\" rx=\"28\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#e7a3a3\" stroke=\"#b5473a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Belly 2</text><line x1=\"214\" y1=\"152\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"254\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"196\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rear chest 2</text><line x1=\"262\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Front chest 2</text><line x1=\"306\" y1=\"128\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Collar 1</text><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"44\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Neck 2</text><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#b5473a\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lung</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Air reaches into the hollow wing bones (80%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>9 air sacs: 4 pairs plus 1 at the base of the neck <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Air even fills the hollow wing bones <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what the sacs do</summary><ul><li>The pairs: neck, front chest, rear chest and belly. The single one sits at the base of the neck (the clavicular sac) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Side pockets from the sacs run into bones: the upper wing bone (humerus), the shoulder girdle, the hips and some neck bones <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Air in the bones and sacs makes the body light for flight <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Divers squeeze air out of the sacs and feathers before a dive (Tip).</li><li>These details come from anatomy studies on pigeons and kingfishers. Ducks follow the same bird plan with small differences <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view of a duck with zones. Green, head, neck and spine: the bird drops at once. Light green, front of the body with heart and lungs: fast. Orange, rear of the body with the big belly air sacs and the gut: slow death, often lost. A wing hit alone: the bird swims and dives. One to two pellets in the vitals kill with the right shot size, 70 percent. Tail end hits cripple, a tip.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Where pellets kill fast, and where they cripple</text><path d=\"M60 160 Q56 120 110 112 L250 108 Q290 106 306 116 Q316 92 334 86 Q358 80 364 98 L378 104 L364 110 Q354 116 340 122 Q332 146 322 168 Q306 206 240 210 L120 210 Q66 204 60 160 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M306 116 Q316 92 334 86 Q358 80 364 98 L378 104 L364 110 Q354 116 340 122 Q332 146 322 168 L290 168 Q292 130 306 116Z\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\" opacity=\".85\"/><path d=\"M200 110 L250 108 Q290 106 306 116 Q292 130 290 168 L322 168 Q306 206 240 210 L200 210 Z\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\" opacity=\".55\"/><path d=\"M60 160 Q56 120 110 112 L200 110 L200 210 L120 210 Q66 204 60 160 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".55\"/><path d=\"M110 118 Q180 98 300 112 Q250 132 140 132 Z\" fill=\"#d9c27a\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"118\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"356\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Head, neck</text><text x=\"356\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">drops now</text><text x=\"252\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heart, lungs</text><text x=\"252\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">fast</text><text x=\"128\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Belly sacs, gut</text><text x=\"128\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">slow, lost</text><text x=\"118\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Wing only:</text><text x=\"118\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">swims, dives</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"226\" width=\"380\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Front half and head: 1 to 2 pellets in the</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">vitals kill, with the right size (70%)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffd8b5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Tail end hits cripple (Tip)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>A pellet in the belly mostly hits air sac and gut, not vitals (Tip).</li><li>Head, neck and the front half kill. Aim at the front <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the shot logic, and how sure we are</summary><ul><li>To kill, a pellet must reach a vital area: brain, spine, or heart and lungs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Published lethality tables count 1 to 2 hits in the vitals with the right pellet size as enough for ducks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>The lungs and heart sit forward and high. A bird hit only in the rear belly can keep flying and die later, unrecovered (Tip, my reading of the anatomy).</li><li>A wing hit alone breaks the bird down but leaves it alive: it swims and dives (Tip).</li><li>Ducks over decoys: Ducks Unlimited advice is to look at and aim at the bill on the first shot <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Lost birds: the commonly quoted figure is about 1 in 5 ducks shot but not recovered in the United States <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Chapter 10 covers pattern, pellet counts and what to do after the shot: <a href=\"#/s/mc-ducks-10\">Masterclass 10</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation, side view of the water. Left: a puddle duck tips forward, head under and tail in the air, then comes back up and tips up again, staying in the shallows. Right: a diving duck goes right under the surface and swims deep and far before it would pop up somewhere else. Final labels: tips up, stays in the shallows; goes right under, pops up far away.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"150\" width=\"400\" height=\"150\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".18\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Puddle duck</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Diver</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"290\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(100 150)\"><g><g transform=\"translate(0 0) scale(1.2)\"><path d=\"M-30 0 Q-34 -14 -18 -14 L10 -12 Q24 -10 24 0 Q20 10 -2 10 L-22 10 Q-32 8 -30 0Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"-20\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M26 -21 L38 -18 L26 -15Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"rotate\" values=\"0;0;75;75;0;0;75\" keyTimes=\"0;.12;.28;.45;.58;.72;1\" dur=\"8s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g></g>\n  <g><g transform=\"translate(0 0) scale(1.0)\"><path d=\"M-30 0 Q-34 -14 -18 -14 L10 -12 Q24 -10 24 0 Q20 10 -2 10 L-22 10 Q-32 8 -30 0Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"-20\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/><path d=\"M26 -21 L38 -18 L26 -15Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/></g><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"290 150;290 150;300 210;340 240;360 250\" keyTimes=\"0;.2;.4;.7;1\" dur=\"8s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"150\" width=\"400\" height=\"150\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".35\"/>\n  <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"7s\" dur=\".6s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"56\" width=\"180\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tips up, stays</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">in the shallows</text><rect x=\"210\" y=\"56\" width=\"180\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Goes right under,</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">pops up far away</text></g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Watch how it feeds: tip up or dive. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>When a diver goes under, its heart slows for a moment <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Forced under, it slows hard to save oxygen <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what happens under water</summary><ul><li>Free diving tufted ducks (a European diver) show a short heart slow down at the start of a dive, then the rate comes back near normal within 6 to 8 seconds <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>On long dives, or when a duck is held under, the heart slows a lot to save oxygen. This drop is stronger in diving ducks than in puddle ducks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: a crippled diver can stay under a long time and come up far away. Most cripples head for the shore and the bank cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Old hand's rule (opinion): \"A wounded diver is a lost diver unless you shoot it on the water now.\"</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mallards-flying.jpg\" alt=\"The breast muscle drives every wingbeat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The breast muscle drives every wingbeat. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jocelyn Anderson, CC BY 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mallard_Ducks_Taking_Off_Over_A_Lake_(185682473).jpeg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>The keel bone anchors the big flight muscles <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>That breast is your meat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the flight engine</summary><ul><li>The keel is a ridge down the middle of the breastbone. The downstroke muscle (pectoralis) and the upstroke muscle (supracoracoideus) both attach to it <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Divers have legs set far back for swimming. That is why they patter along the water to take off and walk badly on land <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Ducks burn about 12 times more energy flying than resting <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Breast meat on ducks is dark red, built for long flights (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Food path in a duck, left to right. Bill: grabs and sieves. Gullet: stretches to hold food. Glandular stomach: acid and enzymes. Gizzard: muscle and grit grind the food like teeth. Gut with two caeca. A mallard gullet can hold up to 113 grams, a quarter pound, of grain, 75 percent. Ducks have no true crop like a grouse, 80 percent. Diving ducks have a bigger gizzard for clams and snails, 75 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Food path: no teeth, a gizzard instead</text><rect x=\"6\" y=\"50\" width=\"72\" height=\"92\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6d7b0\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bill</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">sieves</text><line x1=\"78\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"80.0\" y2=\"96.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"86.0,96.0 75.0,90.0 75.0,102.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"85\" y=\"50\" width=\"72\" height=\"92\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9d7b8\"/><text x=\"121\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gullet</text><text x=\"121\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stretches</text><line x1=\"157\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"159.0\" y2=\"96.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"165.0,96.0 154.0,90.0 154.0,102.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"164\" y=\"50\" width=\"72\" height=\"92\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e7c3b3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Stomach</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">acid</text><line x1=\"236\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"238.0\" y2=\"96.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"244.0,96.0 233.0,90.0 233.0,102.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"243\" y=\"50\" width=\"72\" height=\"92\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#c9a07a\"/><text x=\"279\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gizzard</text><text x=\"279\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">grit grinds</text><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"317.0\" y2=\"96.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"323.0,96.0 312.0,90.0 312.0,102.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"322\" y=\"50\" width=\"72\" height=\"92\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"358\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gut</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2 caeca</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"156\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mallard gullet: up to 113 g of grain (75%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"204\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">No true crop like a grouse (80%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"252\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#cfe0ef\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"278\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Divers: bigger gizzard for clams (75%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>No teeth. The gizzard and grit grind the food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>A mallard can stuff about 113 g of grain in its gullet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: food in, energy out</summary><ul><li>Ducks have no true crop, but the gullet (oesophagus) stretches to hold food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Order: bill, gullet, glandular stomach (proventriculus: acid and enzymes), gizzard (muscle plus grit), gut and two caeca <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>The gizzard grows bigger when a duck eats hard food. Divers that eat clams and snails have bigger gizzards for their size <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Why they gorge fast: eat quickly in the field, then fly to safe water to digest <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: a duck with a fat gullet of grain tells you what field it fed in. Check the gullet when you clean birds (Tip).</li><li>Laying wood duck hens may need more than 300 invertebrates an hour for eight hours <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Leg of a duck standing in cold water. A red artery carries warm blood down; a blue vein carries cool blood back up right beside it. Heat jumps across from the artery to the vein, so the returning blood is warmed and the feet stay near water temperature. Little body heat is lost, 80 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cold feet, warm body (80%)</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"230\" width=\"400\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\".6\"/><path d=\"M150 40 L150 200 L120 236 M150 200 L150 240 M150 200 L180 236\" stroke=\"#d98b2b\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M140 40 L140 196\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"6\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M160 196 L160 40\" stroke=\"#3a6fb0\" stroke-width=\"6\" fill=\"none\"/><line x1=\"142\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"152.0\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"158.0,70.0 147.0,64.0 147.0,76.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><line x1=\"142\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"152.0\" y2=\"110.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"158.0,110.0 147.0,104.0 147.0,116.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><line x1=\"142\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"152.0\" y2=\"150.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"158.0,150.0 147.0,144.0 147.0,156.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><line x1=\"132\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"132.0\" y2=\"184.0\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"132.0,190.0 138.0,179.0 126.0,179.0\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"168\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"168.0\" y2=\"66.0\" stroke=\"#3a6fb0\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"168.0,60.0 162.0,71.0 174.0,71.0\" fill=\"#3a6fb0\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#c0392b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Warm blood down</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heat jumps across</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#3a6fb0\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cool blood back up</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#3a6fb0\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">is warmed again</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"274\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feet stay near water temperature</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Little heat lost (80%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Warm and cold blood pass side by side in the legs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Feet stay cold, the body stays warm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: cold, wind and clear nights</summary><ul><li>The setup is called countercurrent heat exchange. It works so well ducks can stand on ice for hours <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Down under the outer feathers holds the warmth. Preened, waterproof outer feathers keep the water out (Tip).</li><li>Clear, cold nights cost a duck the most energy. Ducks pick sheltered roosts out of the wind <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: on a cold, windy day ducks feed harder and seek calm, sheltered water. Hunt the lee shore (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A general pattern of a duck&#x27;s fat through the year, 75 percent. Spring: hens store fat for eggs. Nesting: hens burn it. Summer moult: low. Fall: fatten before migration. Migration: burn it. Winter: regain on grain in early winter, then a slow drop in mid to late winter. Flying burns about 12 times the energy of resting.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A duck&#x27;s fat through the year (75%)</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fat</text><path d=\"M50 150 L90 120 L130 90 L170 170 L210 200 L250 180 L290 120 L320 160 L350 110 L385 130\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spring:</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">hens store</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nesting</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">burns it</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moult</text><text x=\"288\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fall: fatten</text><text x=\"322\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Migrate:</text><text x=\"322\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">burn it</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Winter</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mar</text><text x=\"124\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text><text x=\"188\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jul</text><text x=\"252\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text><text x=\"316\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"254\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Flying burns about 12 times resting energy</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Fall: they fatten before migration <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hens spend their fat on eggs and nesting <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the fat cycle and the meat</summary><ul><li>Before fall migration ducks eat more and store fat faster <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They reach winter waters fairly lean, then regain fat in early winter on grain and seeds <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>From mid to late winter body mass usually drops, likely set by the body itself <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hens rely on fat, protein and calcium built up over winter and spring for eggs and incubation <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter meaning: late October and November birds are often fat, with a yellow layer under the skin. Pluck them to keep it (Tip). See <a href=\"#/s/cooking/4\">Cooking</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart: of 100 adult mallards, about 63 are alive after 1 year, 40 after 2, 25 after 3, 16 after 4 and 10 after 5, using about 6 in 10 surviving each year, 75 percent. The oldest wild mallard on record lived to 27, 70 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">100 adult mallards: how many left?</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"50.0\" width=\"44\" height=\"160.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"52\" y=\"42.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">100</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Start</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"109.19999999999999\" width=\"44\" height=\"100.80000000000001\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"112\" y=\"101.19999999999999\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">63</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yr 1</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"146.0\" width=\"44\" height=\"64.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"172\" y=\"138.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">40</text><text x=\"172\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yr 2</text><rect x=\"210\" y=\"170.0\" width=\"44\" height=\"40.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"232\" y=\"162.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">25</text><text x=\"232\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yr 3</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"184.4\" width=\"44\" height=\"25.6\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"292\" y=\"176.4\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">16</text><text x=\"292\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yr 4</text><rect x=\"330\" y=\"194.0\" width=\"44\" height=\"16.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"186.0\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">10</text><text x=\"352\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yr 5</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"244\" width=\"380\" height=\"48\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">About 6 in 10 adults live each year (75%)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oldest wild mallard on record: 27 (70%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>About 6 in 10 adult mallards live through a year <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Few wild ducks reach 5 years <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: survival numbers</summary><ul><li>Adult mallard yearly survival in band studies: about 0.6 to 0.66. Young birds survive less <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Of 100 adults, about 63 are left after a year, 40 after two, 10 after five (number trick using 0.63, Tip).</li><li>Oldest known wild mallard: a drake about 27 years old <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Clutch 5 to 15 eggs replaces the losses each year <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Young birds of the year are more likely to be shot than adults <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. More in <a href=\"#/s/mc-ducks-04\">Masterclass 4</a>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"In to the back, out through the lung\"</td><td>One way airflow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>9 sacs: 4 pairs + 1</td><td>Air sacs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>\"Front half, fast. Back half, lost.\"</td><td>Shot placement (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>113 g = a quarter pound</td><td>Mallard gullet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td></tr><tr><td>6 in 10</td><td>Adults alive next year <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Old hand's rule (opinion): \"Shoot the head of the bird, not the bird.\" Ducks Unlimited teaches the same on decoying ducks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</p></aside><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li>\"Gizzard and grit are the duck's teeth.\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>\"Feet like ice, body like a stove.\" Countercurrent exchange <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>\"Fat in the fall, gone by the nest.\" <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Main source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breathing, air sacs</td><td>Peer reviewed anatomy papers</td></tr><tr><td>Digestion, fat</td><td>Ducks Unlimited science pages</td></tr><tr><td>Survival</td><td>Band recovery studies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source, with date checked</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11864838/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Unidirectional airflow, air sacs or the horizontal septum (review, PubMed Central)</a> and <a href=\"https://digfir-published.macmillanusa.com/life11e/life11e_ch48_15.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Life, 11th edition, chapter 48</a>: one way flow, two breath cycle. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://journal.tu.edu.ye/index.php/yjavs/article/view/2596\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gross anatomy of the air sacs of the kingfisher</a> and <a href=\"https://journal.djas.uodiyala.edu.iq/index.php/dasj/article/view/3518\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">air sacs of pigeons</a>: 9 air sacs, bones aired. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://ucmp.berkeley.edu/diapsids/birds/birdmm.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aves: more on morphology, UC Museum of Paleontology</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keel_(bird_anatomy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Keel (bird anatomy)</a>): keel and flight muscles. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://TPWD.texas.gov/huntwild/wild/game_management/media/roster_nontoxic_shot.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tom Roster's 2012 nontoxic shot lethality table</a>: 1 to 2 hits. Read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://kb.rspca.org.au/knowledge-base/how-many-ducks-and-quail-are-wounded-due-to-recreational-hunting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RSPCA knowledge base on duck wounding</a>: vital areas brain, heart and lungs. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.outdoorlife.com/deep-dives/waterfowl-crippling-rates-hunting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outdoor Life, waterfowl crippling rates</a>: 20% federal estimate, cripples head for shore. Read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/hunting/shooting-tips/shotgunning-ducks-over-decoys\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited, shotgunning ducks over decoys</a>: aim at the bill. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Butler and colleagues, Journal of Experimental Biology: <a href=\"https://cob.silverchair.com/jeb/article/79/1/283/22611/Changes-in-Heart-Rate-and-Respiratory-Frequency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heart rate in free diving tufted ducks (1979)</a> and <a href=\"https://cob.silverchair.com/jeb/article-pdf/138/1/195/2638521/jexbio_138_1_195.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tufted duckling diving (1988)</a>. Search preview 2026-10-11 (75 to 80%).</li><li><a href=\"https://ducks.org/conservation/waterfowl-research-science/understanding-waterfowl-duck-digestion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited, Understanding waterfowl: duck digestion</a>: gullet, gizzard, caeca, 300 invertebrates. Read directly 2026-10-11 (75 to 80%).</li><li><a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/magazine/how-birds-keep-their-feet-freezing-frigid-temperatures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audubon, how birds keep their feet from freezing</a>. Read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://ducks.org/conservation/waterfowl-research-science/ducks-after-dark\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited, Ducks after dark</a>: clear night energy cost, sheltered roosts. Read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/conservation/waterfowl-research-science/understanding-waterfowl-survival-of-the-fattest\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited, Survival of the fattest</a>: fall fattening, 12 times flight cost, winter loss. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://bioone.org/journals/journal-of-field-ornithology/volume-74/issue-1/0273-8570-74.1.1/Survival-and-recovery-of-Mallards-and-Gadwalls-banded-in-eastern/10.1648/0273-8570-74.1.1.full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Giudice 2003, survival of mallards and gadwalls, Journal of Field Ornithology</a>: 0.56 to 0.66. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://nwf.org/Educational-Resources/Wildlife-Guide/Birds/Mallard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">National Wildlife Federation, mallard</a>: oldest 27, clutch 5 to 15. Search preview 2026-10-11 (70 to 75%).</li><li><a href=\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70243783\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bellrose and others, sex and age ratios in North American ducks (1961)</a>: young birds more vulnerable. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]